Task 1: Switching SELinux Modes Using setenforce
Subtask 1.1: Check Current SELinux Status
Before modifying SELinux, verify its current status.

Run the following command to check SELinux status:

sestatus
Expected Output:

SELinux status:                 enabled  
SELinuxfs mount:                /sys/fs/selinux  
SELinux root directory:         /etc/selinux  
Loaded policy name:             targeted  
Current mode:                   enforcing  
Mode from config file:          enforcing  
Alternatively, use:

getenforce
Expected Output:

Enforcing  
Subtask 1.2: Temporarily Change SELinux Mode
Use setenforce to switch between Enforcing and Permissive modes.

To switch to Permissive mode (logs violations but does not enforce them):

sudo setenforce 0
Verify:

getenforce
Expected Output:

Permissive  
To switch back to Enforcing mode:

sudo setenforce 1
Verify again:

getenforce
Expected Output:

Enforcing  
Note: Changes made with setenforce are temporary and reset after reboot.

Subtask 1.3: Permanently Change SELinux Mode
To make changes persistent, modify /etc/selinux/config.

Open the config file:

sudo vi /etc/selinux/config
Change the SELINUX= line to:

SELINUX=permissive  
(or enforcing if reverting).

Save and exit.

Reboot the system for changes to take effect:

sudo reboot
Task 2: Managing SELinux Policies with semanage
Subtask 2.1: Install policycoreutils-python-utils
Ensure semanage is available:

sudo dnf install policycoreutils-python-utils -y
Subtask 2.2: List SELinux Port Contexts
View current port assignments:

sudo semanage port -l
Expected Output:

SELinux Port Type              Proto    Port Number  
http_port_t                    tcp      80, 443, 8080  
Subtask 2.3: Add a Custom Port to SELinux Policy
If an application uses a non-standard port (e.g., 8081 for HTTP), add it:

sudo semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8081
Verify:

sudo semanage port -l | grep 8081
Expected Output:

http_port_t                    tcp      8081  
Subtask 2.4: Remove a Custom Port
To remove the port:

sudo semanage port -d -t http_port_t -p tcp 8081
Task 3: Troubleshooting SELinux Denials
Subtask 3.1: Check SELinux Denials in Logs
Use ausearch to check denials:

sudo ausearch -m avc -ts recent
Alternatively, check /var/log/audit/audit.log:

sudo grep "avc: denied" /var/log/audit/audit.log
Subtask 3.2: Generate and Apply Custom Policy Module
If a service is denied access, generate a policy:

Use audit2allow to create a module:

sudo grep "avc: denied" /var/log/audit/audit.log | audit2allow -M mypolicy
Apply the module:

sudo semodule -i mypolicy.pp
Subtask 3.3: Temporarily Allow Access (Permissive Mode for Debugging)
If unsure about policy changes, switch to Permissive mode:

sudo setenforce 0
Test the application, then revert:

sudo setenforce 1
